## 0.75ct Princess vs 0.75ct Cushion and Oval

Princess is not the only fancy shape at 0.75ct. Two other shapes compete directly at this carat weight and price point.

**0.75ct princess vs 0.75ct cushion:**

A 0.75ct cushion brilliant is approximately 5.0–5.4mm — similar face-up to princess at this carat weight. Cushion cut costs 10–15% more than princess at the same grade because of its more forgiving clarity grading (inclusions are less visible in cushion's broken-up facet pattern). If the square shape appeals but corner protection feels like a daily maintenance concern, cushion is worth considering.

The tradeoff: cushion is slightly more expensive, slightly less precise in corners, and creates a softer, more romantic look versus princess's sharp geometric lines.

**0.75ct princess vs 0.75ct oval:**

A 0.75ct oval is approximately 7.0×5.0mm — oval's elongated shape covers more finger length than princess's 5.0×5.0mm square. Oval at 0.75ct appears larger than princess at 0.75ct due to the longer silhouette. Oval costs roughly similar to princess at the same grade.

Choose oval if finger length coverage matters. Choose princess if you specifically want the square geometric look.

**The shape priority checklist at 0.75ct:**

1. If you want square: princess (not cushion — cushion is softer and rounder)
2. If you want maximum face coverage: oval (longer apparent face-up than either square shape)
3. If you want the sharpest, most architectural look: princess

The 0.75ct size works well in all three shapes. The shape decision should come before the carat weight decision — choose the shape you love, then find the best stone in that shape at your budget.

