## My Final Verdict — The 0.75ct Princess Decision

The 0.75ct princess cut is the most strategically underrated size in the princess market. It is 90% of the face-up area of 1ct at 63% of the 1ct price — and nobody at normal social distance can tell the difference.

Three buying rules at 0.75ct:

1. **Consider 0.74ct.** The threshold premium at 0.75ct is 12–16%. A 0.74ct G-VS1 is indistinguishable from 0.75ct on a hand.
2. **VS1 minimum.** The $175–$200 clarity upgrade from VS2 to VS1 at this size is the most reliable money spent in the 0.75ct budget.
3. **G color minimum in white metals.** Same rule as at 1ct. H color at 0.75ct is more forgiving than at 1ct or 2ct, but G is still the correct floor.

What the 0.75ct princess market rewards is patience. Buyers who filter by proportions first, check the GIA plot manually for corner inclusions, and compare VS1 vs VS2 side by side consistently outperform buyers who filter by price alone. The ~$150–$200 clarity upgrade from VS2 to VS1 and the ~$150–$200 carat threshold saving from 0.74ct instead of 0.75ct together offset each other — you end up spending the same money but getting a cleaner, more reliable stone.

The size is genuinely competitive at this market moment. The Three-Quarter Sweet Spot exists because round cut buyers skip it on the way to 1ct, lab-grown buyers skip it for bigger lab stones at lower prices, and the result is a thin natural market with excellent GIA stones available at honest prices.

**Best pick:** GIA 0.74ct G-VS1 Ideal princess at approximately $1,380 — below the magic threshold, VS1 clarity, G color, and $832 less than the 1ct equivalent while delivering 90% of the face-up area. Add a $510 14K white gold V-prong solitaire for a complete ring under $2,000.

